#edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1045 "Domain and Range of Quadratic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124689 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "54746d4e-16f7-5527-9c6f-def91a1f4b6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124866 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124866 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124866 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124866 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)={(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124866 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1930d180-4cc3-5e28-910d-7be8bef132f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124866 #edb/ref 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-\\frac{5}{6}$ and $k=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the vertex is $(-\\frac{5}{6},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since $a=1>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$ \n\n![](images/54/54e0b0ea5dd498a36fc854781dc5120ce1de9bee0c333eecc6d895ce09b72424.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124930 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=(x+6)^{2}-6?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b78d132-27ad-5a99-9537-835ebfa56a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124930 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadratic function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125237 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125237 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125237 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125237 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+5?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125237 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8151bd28-84e4-5d59-ba0b-4c6824e316e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125237 #edb/ref 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=5.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-4)}{2\\cdot 2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(1)+5 \\\\  & =2-4+5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,3),$ and the range of the function is $[3,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/a8/a8fc15df2b24054485bb07330f40919ebf13cb9969073a733b9426a28dab20b5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125509 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125509 #edb/ref 1061 29837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125509 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e12eeac1-466a-5f3e-b414-d25fd0ed1c89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125509 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125512 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125512 #edb/ref 1061 29974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125512 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7d9de02-5d84-5776-9397-cf6b426b9eb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125512 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125559 #edb/ref 1061 32412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd9056e9-1e4b-5477-b146-89ad0b007f19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125663 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125663 #edb/ref 1061 4602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "382f3a3b-5fd1-5d9c-acfc-d0673b1bdd60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125664 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125664 #edb/ref 1061 4604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125664 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "967be501-fa9c-5999-83c9-e020f0c1112d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125664 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125665 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125665 #edb/ref 1061 4609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125665 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0db717c3-7b6a-5b97-8fd1-af369e6ff4d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125665 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125666 #edb/ref 1061 4610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87f0d6e4-1968-5bc6-bd74-da8ebb01d739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125716 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will discuss the properties of quadratic functions. Specifically, we learn how to determine the domain and range of a quadratic function.\n\nRemember that a quadratic function takes the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = ax^2 + bx + c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nThe first thing to note is that the domain of *any* quadratic function is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ because it is defined for all values of $x.$\n\nDetermining the range of a quadratic function is slightly more involved, as we'll see shortly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125716 #edb/ref 1065 1649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125716 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "754107de-231b-53d3-9c87-0c63f43c0733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125716 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125717 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider a parabola that opens downwards ($a<0$). In this case, there is no minimum output, but the maximum output corresponds to the $y$-coordinate of the vertex. Therefore, if the vertex is $(h,k),$ then the range is $y \\leq k.$\n\nFor example, in the graph below, the $y$-value of the vertex is $y=2$, so the range of the function is $y \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4390b9cec5d5fce67691421479151be3592967f95d22ddde307c6d56c630879.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125717 #edb/ref 1065 1651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125717 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "886809b2-0204-52ae-8f7b-c83f91d56f41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125717 #edb/ref 1067 "The Range of a Downward-Opening Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125846 #edb/ref 1064 "The range of a quadratic function depends on the direction in which its graph opens. Regardless of whether the equation is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c,\n$$\n\nvertex form, \n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k,\n$$\n\nor factored form,\n\n$$\ny = a(x-x_1)(x-x_2),\n$$\n\nit opens upwards if the leading coefficient $a$ is positive and downwards if $a$ is negative.\n\nNow, consider a parabola that opens upwards ($a>0$). In this case, the minimum output corresponds to the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, while there is no maximum output. Therefore, if the vertex is $(h,k),$ then the range is $y \\geq k.$\n\nFor example, in the graph below, the $y$-value of the vertex is $y=-2$, so the range of the function is $y \\geq -2.$\n\n![](images/ac/ac254aaa0bdab2999e07c46f82becd37fb4b8ee6e816ad91842d6e750eb90b86.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125846 #edb/ref 1065 5155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125846 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "505edbed-bec8-50a0-8a52-67e25a936784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125846 #edb/ref 1067 "The Range of an Upward-Opening Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549361 #edb/ref 1080 "[0,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549361 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549361 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17490e92-f04a-51f7-b438-199eeb712801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549401 #edb/ref 1080 "[1,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549401 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549401 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28ae418f-d853-574e-aad6-2711015f76d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549425 #edb/ref 1080 "[0,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549425 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549425 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3342ac6c-a5ac-505a-9057-009d9aaad1b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549534 #edb/ref 1080 "(-\\infty ,1]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549534 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549534 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "720e13e8-4fcd-54db-bf77-f1e56dad5e3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549593 #edb/ref 1080 "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549593 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549593 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e98df84-9ac6-56f7-ab33-a824ee4007cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549686 #edb/ref 1080 "(-\\infty ,3]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549686 #edb